  "abstract": "This Python code implements a graph-based reduction framework designed for the management and analysis of drinking water distribution networks. It was developed within the framework of the ANR CoRREau project, which focuses on improving the protection, robustness, and resilience of these critical infrastructures.The code relies on the OOPNET library to import, process, and manipulate EPANET INP files, which contain the topological and hydraulic characteristics of water distribution systems.The proposed methodology is based on graph decomposition techniques, particularly the Forest Core Partitioning and Graph Matrix Partitioning algorithms. These approaches aim to split the hydraulic system equations by separately treating the linear and nonlinear components that describe the behaviour of water distribution networks. This decomposition enables more efficient analysis, simplification, and reduction of complex network models while preserving their essential hydraulic properties.",
